\begin{abstract}

\noindent The automated categorization, phylogenetic arrangement, and hierarchical structuring of large-scale digital typography repositories present complex challenges at the intersection of computer vision, software engineering, and computational aesthetics. Modern publishing pipelines frequently ingest heterogeneous archives comprising thousands of TrueType (\texttt{.ttf}), OpenType (\texttt{.otf}), and TrueType Collection (\texttt{.ttc}) files. This paper delivers an exhaustive methodological critique and comparative framework analyzing two opposing classification paradigms: (1) traditional deterministic parsing relying on embedded binary metadata tables and geometric vector heuristics, and (2) modern Artificial Intelligence (AI) methodologies leveraging deep Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and unsupervised latent space manifold projection. We formalize the mathematical pipelines of both approaches, examine critical edge cases such as CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) multi-byte glyph sets and variable font axes, and

al pipelines of both approaches, examine critical edge cases such as CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) multi-byte glyph sets and variable font axes, and evaluate fault tolerance against metadata corruption. Authored collaboratively as an open human-AI inquiry, this document serves as a foundational preprint designed explicitly to solicit rigorous methodological critique from the computational design and machine learning communities prior to empirical deployment.

\end{abstract}

\vspace{1cm}

\section{Introduction and Problem Statement}

Typography serves as the foundational visual infrastructure of human communication. Historically, the systematization of letterform morphology has relied on manual, expert-driven classification frameworks, most notably the Vox-ATypI taxonomy, which categorizes typefaces into distinct historical and structural lineages (e.g., \textit{Humanist}, \textit{Garalde}, \textit{Didone}, \textit{Geometric Sans}). However, the democratization of digital font creation and the exponential growth of open-source foundries have resulted in repositories containing tens of thousands of unique typefaces.

