\begin{abstract}

Browser-based scientific visualization has traditionally been constrained by single-precision IEEE 754 floating-point limits, host-to-device memory transfer overhead, and spatial aliasing on high-frequency recursive boundaries. This paper formalizes a self-contained, zero-dependency monolithic Progressive Web Application (PWA) architecture for real-time fractal rendering in WebGL 2.0, instantiated in the public Fractinity system (\url{https://fract.kleynenberg.com/} and \url{https://fractinity.netlify.app/}). By encapsulating the application shell, offline service-worker caching, and GLSL fragment shader pipelines into a single client-side bundle, the architecture eliminates server round-trips and PCIe framebuffer transfers during interactive exploration. To overcome single-precision zoom saturation beyond $10^7$, the Mandelbrot pipeline employs perturbation theory ($\delta_{n+1} = 2z_n\delta_n + \delta_n^2 + \delta_c$) around a high-precision reference orbit, enabling parallel 32-bit fragment evaluation across the viewport. In

